;; agent$init (RFC-040): /init. Makes a directory a project brainyard works
;; well in — the tools checked with how to install the missing ones, AGENTS.md
;; and a CLAUDE.md that imports it, beads set up by bd — and drafts the
;; project's own section of AGENTS.md.
;;
;; Its own loop is the whole agent (brainyard/loops/init.edn): no model turn of
;; its own calls a tool, so its tools are the init$* commands alone, and its
;; :config is what registers them.
;;
;; brainyard's /init authors .brainyard/BRAINYARD.md instead (init-agent):
;; divergence `init-sets-up-the-project`.
{:description
 "Prepares this project for brainyard: checks the tools brainyard relies on (git, bd, tmux, claude, gh, codex) and says how to install the missing ones; writes AGENTS.md and a CLAUDE.md that imports it, keeping any text already there; sets up beads with bd; drafts the project's section of AGENTS.md from the repository; when the person says what the project is for, drafts its profile (goal, scope, done criteria, how far agents go on their own) and writes it only on their yes. Say \"check\" to only report. Installs a tool only when asked to, and only after the person says yes."

 :instruction-resource "brainyard/prompts/agents/init.md"

 :loop "brainyard/loops/init.edn"

 :tools ["init$*"]
 :deny-tools ["agent$*"]

 ;; it asks the person as it works, so its call is waited on, never detached
 :attended? true

 :tier :standard

 :config {:init-tools? true
          :tool-authoring? false
          :hook-authoring? false
          :user-tools? false}}
